# Line endings for the files that run on Linux.
#
# This machine has `core.autocrlf=true`, which rewrites LF to CRLF in the working tree on
# checkout. That is right for editing on Windows and wrong for everything below, all of which is
# executed inside a container or on the VPS:
#
#   * a shell script whose shebang line ends in CR fails with `bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env
#     bash^M` — a message that names the interpreter and not the real cause, which is why this
#     costs an hour the first time.
#   * `.env` parsed by Docker Compose keeps the CR as part of the VALUE, so SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY
#     arrives with a trailing carriage return and every authenticated request is refused with no
#     hint as to why.
#
# `eol=lf` forces LF in the working tree regardless of core.autocrlf, so `scp` from Windows and
# `git clone` on the server both produce a file that runs.
#
# Nothing else in the repository is listed. Source files are Vite's business and their line
# endings have never mattered.

*.sh        text eol=lf
Dockerfile  text eol=lf
*.inc       text eol=lf
Caddyfile   text eol=lf
*.example   text eol=lf
compose*.yaml text eol=lf
deploy/nginx.conf text eol=lf
